      "side": 1,
      "message": "You don\u0027t need to define it when setting. From my understanding we would have to set it internally to our current pid when setting a lock. Then if we get a lock and find a conflict (ie lock with overlap and different pid) the pid field on the returned lock would correspond to the pid of the process that set the conflicting lock. In theory this means that the returned lock should never equal our current process lock.",
